<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="billing"
      updated="August 18, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"plans", "Plans and limits"},
        {"entitlements", "What each plan unlocks"},
        {"usage-and-limits", "When you reach a limit"},
        {"changing-plan", "Upgrading and downgrading"},
        {"invoices", "Invoices and payment method"},
        {"payment-failure", "If a payment fails"},
        {"retention", "Plan changes and audit retention"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="billing">
        <:dek>
          Your plan sets this account's hard limits — how many runners connect, how many people you
          can invite, how long the audit log is kept, and which features are on. Billing runs on
          Paddle; you manage it from
          <a
            href={~p"/app/billing"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Billing</a>
          under Settings.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="plans">Plans and limits</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar has three plans. A new account is on Free until someone upgrades it, and the plan
        comes from your subscription — there's no separate plan switch on the account to drift out
        of sync. Each plan sets three hard limits.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="border-b border-zinc-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Plan</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Runners</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Team members</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Audit retention</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">Free</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">3</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">1</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">7 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">Team</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">100</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">Unlimited</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">90 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">Enterprise</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">Unlimited</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">Unlimited</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">365 days</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Free needs no card and has no time limit. Team is billed per enabled runner, monthly or
        annually. Annual billing costs about two months less than monthly billing. Checkout uses
        the current enabled-runner count, with a minimum quantity of one. Enterprise is a custom,
        sales-led plan with tailored limits. For the current per-runner price, <.link
          href={~p"/pricing" <> "#compare"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >compare the plans</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="entitlements">What each plan unlocks</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Some features also depend on the plan. Free includes the console, audit trail, policies,
        approvals, and runbooks. Paid plans add features for teams and external systems.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="border-b border-zinc-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Feature</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Available on</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">Single sign-on (OIDC)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top whitespace-nowrap">Team and Enterprise</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">Audit export (CSV + SIEM)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top whitespace-nowrap">Team and Enterprise</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">SCIM directory sync</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top whitespace-nowrap">Enterprise</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Single sign-on.</strong>
            OIDC sign-in through your identity provider. See <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >single sign-on &amp; SCIM</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Audit export.</strong>
            Every plan keeps the full audit trail in the console. CSV download and the NDJSON API
            export that data. Export is a paid feature. See <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >audit &amp; SIEM</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM directory sync.</strong>
            Provision and deprovision members from your directory. Enterprise also includes a
            security and procurement review, deployment planning, and rollout support from our
            team.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="usage-and-limits">When you reach a limit</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner and member caps are enforced when you add the next one, not retroactively — so a
        plan never silently disables something you already have.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Runners.</strong>
            Enrolling a runner past your plan's cap is refused. The new host's registration fails
            with an over-limit error instead of half-joining. Existing enabled runners keep their
            current state and can continue executing actions.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team members.</strong>
            Inviting a member past the cap is refused the same way (Free allows one, so the second
            person needs Team); people already in the account keep their access.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Watch it before you hit it.</strong>
            The Billing page shows live usage against each cap — a runner meter and a member meter
            that turn amber as you approach the limit — so you see the wall coming rather than
            discovering it when the next install fails.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Audit retention</strong>
            is a duration limit. Events older than their fixed retention window are pruned. Plan
            changes affect new events only. See <.link
              href="#retention"
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >below</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="changing-plan">Upgrading and downgrading</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Anyone in the account can open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/billing"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Billing</a>
        and read the plan, its limits, and how much of them is in use.
        An owner, admin, or billing manager can change the plan, update the card, or cancel. Those
        roles can also open the plan catalog and read invoices. Operators and viewers cannot. See <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >roles</.link>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To upgrade, open <a
          href={~p"/app/billing"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Settings → Billing</a>, pick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Monthly</strong>
        or <strong class="text-zinc-200">Annual</strong>, and choose a plan. Checkout opens in a
        Paddle overlay on the page. After payment clears, you return to Billing. The new plan and
        its limits take effect after Paddle confirms the charge.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Use the Paddle customer portal to downgrade or cancel. Open <a
          href={~p"/app/billing"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Settings → Billing</a>, use <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manage subscription</strong>
        to open the portal. Do not start another checkout for this change.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Cancellation status is advisory">
        <p>
          A cancellation does not remove emisar plan access today. The current plan and limits stay
          active until the mirrored subscription plan changes. Billing shows the cancellation date
          from Paddle.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Who can change billing.">
        Owners and admins can manage billing. Either can grant the billing-manager role. A billing
        manager handles the subscription, payment method, and invoices. The role can also read the
        member list and billing audit events. It cannot access runners, policies, or actions. Give
        it to the person who owns invoices but not infrastructure.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="invoices">Invoices and payment method</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The Billing page shows recent invoice dates, amounts, numbers, and status. Each invoice has
        a PDF download. You can check the latest charge without leaving the console.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open the Paddle customer portal for full invoice history, payment methods, billing
        addresses, and tax details. Start from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/billing"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Settings → Billing</a>
        and choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manage subscription</strong>. Paddle is emisar's merchant
        of record. It owns the complete ledger and card details.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Manage subscription appears after Paddle creates a customer for the account. This happens
        after the first paid-plan checkout. A Free account without that checkout has nothing to
        manage.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="payment-failure">If a payment fails</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A failed renewal does not lock you out. Paddle retries the charge and emails the billing
        contact. Your current plan and limits stay active because billing status is advisory.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a
          href={~p"/app/billing"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Settings → Billing</a>, choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manage subscription</strong>,
        and update the payment method. Paddle uses it for the next retry. If you want to stop
        renewal, cancel in the Paddle portal. Every plan change is written to the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >audit log</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="retention">Plan changes and audit retention</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each audit event gets a fixed retention deadline when it is written. The deadline is 7 days
        on Free, 90 on Team, and 365 on Enterprise. A plan change affects only later events.
        Upgrades keep new history longer. Downgrades shorten the window for new events without
        deleting older retained history. Retention pruning runs daily. Plan changes and prunes are
        recorded in the audit log.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="Ingest ahead of the window.">
        If you keep audit history longer than your plan's retention, poll it into your SIEM before
        the horizon passes. Then how long it is kept becomes your SIEM's policy, not ours. The
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}>export API</.link>
        is built for exactly this.
      </.docs_callout>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
